# DTF tier manifest: oci-blob-journal-per-repo
#   artifact-keeper#3851 (PR #4301, 1.11.0): the OCI upload cleanup journal is
#   scoped per (repository, storage key), and a repository delete on a
#   shared-namespace backend no longer purges journaled `oci-blobs/<digest>`
#   objects another repository may hold.
#   Profile-set: s3 (MinIO).
#
# THE BUG
# `oci_upload_cleanup_keys.storage_key` was UNIQUE across the database and
# blob keys are content-addressed, so two pushes of one digest to different
# repositories shared ONE journal row.
#  (a) Repository B registers the row for `oci-blobs/<digest>` at completion;
#      repository A pushes the same digest and its commit deletes the shared
#      row; A is then deleted (its oci_blobs row goes). B's commit-time claim
#      finds its row gone and no peer oci_blobs row to prove a peer cleared
#      it, and refuses a valid push: 503 BLOB_UPLOAD_INVALID "blob storage
#      was being reclaimed concurrently".
#  (b) On S3/GCS/Azure all repositories share one `oci-blobs/` namespace.
#      Deleting a repository purged every key in its journal, so a lingering
#      row of an interrupted A completion made the delete remove the object
#      repository B had committed: B's blob answers 404.
# The fix: rows unique per (repository_id, storage_key), per-key advisory
# lock, and on cloud backends a repository delete records the journaled blob
# keys in oci_gc_candidates (deleted after a 24 h grace once unreferenced)
# instead of deleting them.
#
# HOW THE WINDOWS ARE OPENED (deterministically, from outside)
#  (a) B's chunked session is completed with an empty final PUT while MinIO is
#      `docker pause`d: the handler registers B's journal row for the blob key
#      and then blocks in the S3 CopyObject. Meanwhile A, a repository pinned
#      to `storage_backend: filesystem` (so it does not need MinIO, and its
#      journal key `oci-blobs/<digest>` is the same string), pushes the digest
#      and is deleted. MinIO is unpaused and B's completion runs its claim.
#  (b) A's completion is interrupted the same way and the backend is
#      restarted while it is blocked, which leaves A's registered blob-key
#      row behind (an abandoned upload). B had already committed the digest.
#      Then A is deleted.
#
# Gates: (CONTROL) a monolithic push to an S3 repository is pullable
# byte-exact; the filesystem repository accepts the same digest; the
# interrupted completion leaves A's journal row behind and the object in
# MinIO (both images: the precondition of (b) is reached). (FIX) B's
# completion is 201 and the blob is pullable byte-exact; B's journal row
# survives A's commit; after A's delete the `oci-blobs/<digest>` object is
# still in MinIO, B pulls it byte-exact, and oci_gc_candidates records the
# key.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: not under test.
#
# Red: ak-backend:1.10.0. Green: ak-backend:1.11.0 and main.
PROFILES="storage.s3"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
